A||||24||Ray Heath||Camaro V6 in a Fiero||||
Z||000000||Ray Heath||05-15-99||06:49 PM||||Does anyone know if the 3.1 or 3.4 from a Camaro will work in the Fiero. It seems like I have seen somewhere were you have to drill the Starter Mount holes on the other side of the block.
Z||000001||SCCA FIERO||05-15-99||10:40 PM||||The 3.1 and 3.4 are common transplants for the fiero. The 3.1 can be simplified by adding a 3.1 crank to the 2.8. Pisa(www.cybercars.com/fierohq.html) has a "stroker" kit that costs $1,000 auto or $1,200 manual.About the same as the 2.8 rebuild and makes the mill more reliable. The 3.4 TDC(twin dual cam) is also a common swap. The 3.4 gives about 215 hp and as i understand it is hard to fit in the Fiero bay. The install kit costs about $1,500 and is available from ACE (American Custom Engineering) 970-259-4156.
Z||000002||Ray Heath||05-16-99||05:20 PM||||The 3.4 OHV engine used in the '93 Camaro is very similar to the 2.8 in the Fiero. That is<br>the one I am concerned with, not the 3.4 TDC<br>used in the Lumina Z34 or Monte Carlo. It looks like it would be an easier upgrade, it<br>even uses the same 884 H.O. heads as the Fiero. Does anyone know anything about this<br>upgrade. Somewhere on the net, I saw where you had to re-drill the Starter Mounting Holes on the other side of the motor, but I can not find the article again. 
Z||000003||Carrolles||05-17-99||05:37 PM||||Ed Parks does this conversion at the Fiero Factory (Fierofactory@juno.com).<br>The 93 through 95 Camaro/Firebird 3.4 ohv engine will look exactily like the Fiero 2.8 when you are finished.<br>You have to move the starter to the other side and if you have auto trans you'll have to grind some metal away to clear the trans.<br>You also have to fabricate a bracket for the lower strut.  This can be done by modifying the A/C bracket.  I believe you also have to either install the 3.4 injectors or use yours with a variable pressure regulator for the fuel system.<p>Call Ed at (256) 420-5391 and he will explain the details.<br>I plan on diong the same thing to my 85SE.<p>Good luck,<br>Carroll
Z||000004||SCCA FIERO||05-17-99||06:57 PM||||Isn't the 3.4 "push rod" motor kinda weak in hp? I mean isn't it prolly better to stroke the 2.8? I read that the 3.4 was designed for economy. What is the hp for the 3.4 "push rod"?
Z||000005||theogre||05-17-99||07:31 PM||||nearly all Fiero compatible v6's are "pushrod" motors. with the obvious exception of some very expensive over head cam rigs.<p>there are problems with both systems.
Z||000006||Carrolles||05-17-99||10:33 PM||||The 93 to 95 camaro 3.4 engine should be around 205 to 215 hp with a lot more low end torque than the stock 2.8.<br>It is about the most cost effective hp increase for the Fiero.<br>Take care,<br>Carroll
Z||000007||LEE498||05-17-99||10:47 PM||||You say the most cost effective hp for <br>the fiero? Nah.... I don't think so!!! I<br>think addind a 3.1 crank and pistons <br>mild cam and port work to the 2.8 is the<br>most cost effective hp for the fiero.  
Z||000008||Carrolles||05-18-99||07:18 AM||||Could be.  I thought stroking the 2.8 with those mods got you to around 190 hp and costs around $1000 to do.  At least that is what a stroker kit costs from places likt the Fiero store and FOCOA.<p>You can pick up Camaro 3.4L engines around here with fewer than 50000 miles for around $500.  Using the heads and intake from the Fiero motor and moving the starter over to the other side of the motor gets you around 205 hp and loads of low end torque.<p>Take care,<br>Carroll
Z||000009||Vincent Whitright||06-02-99||07:30 PM||||I saw someone in this thread post a comment that the 3.4 uses the same heads as the 2.8. Is this true? And if this is the case, isn't it the same block as the 2.8 and 3.1? If so, why would it be harder to fit in the engine compartment? And lastly, can a 3.4 crank fit a 3.1 just as a 3.1 will fit a 2.8?
Z||000010||batboy||06-20-99||08:09 AM||||The 2.8, 3.1, and 3.4 (single cam) engines are all in the same "60 degree V6 family" Heads are interchangable as is most external stuff, with a few exceptions.<p>The 3.4 liter was made for front engine/rear wheel drive cars, so that's why there are problems with swapping it into a Fiero, which uses a front wheel drive engine. As mentioned earlier, the starter is on the opposite side and must be relocated, etc. If you're still interested in swapping in a 3.4, GM makes a complete performance crate engine that you can buy over the counter. It is offered primarily as a replacement motor for Camaros and S-10's. Check out this link for more info on this performance crate engine:  www.sallee-chevrolet.com<p>The 3.1 and 3.4 uses the same crankshaft. The main internal difference in the two motors is that the 3.4 has a larger diameter bore. The block is beefer to allow this, so you can't just bore out a 2.8 or 3.1 to the size of the 3.4 without hitting the water jacket.<p>The cast iron 3.1 liter engines from the early 90's front-wheel drive GM minivans will bolt right up to a Fiero and use all the existing Fiero engine accessories and parts. About the only thing to change is to use an adjustable fuel pressure regulator and a neutrally balanced flywheel/flexplate. If you could pick up one of these cheap enough and if it was a real good, low-mileage engine, that might save you the trouble and expense of having to completely rebuild your tired old 2.8 motor.
Z||000011||mwbackus||06-20-99||08:30 PM||||I plan to do this upgrade in the future and my research has been somewhat successful.  Here is a link to a page describing a 225hp - 250hp build on a 3.4 with lots of part numbers, etc.:<p>http://www.angelfire.com/on/freddysplace/Page1.html<p>And of course the actual web page for Ed's Fiero Factory:<p>http://www.angelfire.com/on/fierofactory/start.html<p><br>I believe that Dennis LaGrua current project is a turbo charged "camaro" 3.4 with 300hp anticipated.  He previously built a "homemade" sub $1k turbo 2.8 that produced around 200hp so I imagine he will be successful.<p>http://members.xoom.com/dlagrua/turbo.htm<p>
Z||000012||batboy||06-20-99||09:35 PM||||I checked out that first link. I seriously think that his goal of 250 horses with what he's using is being overly optimistic. Probably more like 225 ponies according to my calculations. Doesn't gain much torque either, maybe 15 ft/lbs at most. He's going to run 9.7:1 compression ratio with ported cast iron heads. That's too high of a C/R to use normal with available pump gas. He'll have to use an octane booster or mix racing fuel all the time. That's a lot of expense and hassle for only a little extra gain in power over a much cheaper and more streetable 3.2, plus you don't have to convert as much to swap it in. I sure wouldn't build one like that for the street. By the way, that Crane cam is very similar to the GM Hi-Po cam. If you guys really have to get the high horsepower numbers, build a 3.2 with 9:1 C/R and spend the money you saved on a roller cam. Now that will give you a streetable 240 to 250 horsepower (using similar cam specs), plus you'll be able to use pump gas and it might even still be cheaper than that 3.4 liter setup.
Z||000013||mwbackus||06-21-99||12:35 AM||||I am sorry, but I do not understand why it is possible to obtain 250hp from a 3.2, and with the same level of components you can not achieve this with a 3.4.  The compression ratio for the 3.2 build you refer to is even lower than that calculated for Freds 3.4 project, so I would conclude that the 3.2 would also need racing fuel?  BTW, thanks for the link to Sallee Chevrolet, I found some very useful information there on V8's.  The new fast burn heads would be pretty nice on a stroked 383 block.
Z||000014||PaUL||06-21-99||03:37 AM||||is a 9.7:1 compression ratio really too high? Ive seen production cars with higher. the stock c/r is 8.something:1, 9.7 doesnt seem that much higher and remember, the higher the c/r the more power you'll get.  
Z||000015||batboy||06-21-99||06:06 AM||||Mwbackus, the high output 3.2 engine I was referring would be using a hydraulic ROLLER cam with lower compression ratio (C/R). A roller cam is far more expensive than a regular hydraulic flat tappet cam. You must also use roller lifters and beef up the heads with heavier valve springs and hi-po screw-in rocker arm studs. Might as well go with the true roller rocker arms too (not roller tip rockers). There are several reasons why a roller cam produces more power from similar specs. Number one reason is less friction. The second is that a roller cam has ramps that are much steeper. It opens the valve to the full open position faster, holds it open longer, and then slams the valve closed faster. Hence why you have to beef up the valve train. They make a bunch more power, but again, they are very expensive and very hard to find for V6's.<p>PaUL, you're right about production cars used to having much higher C/R's, but those times are long gone. Yes, increasing C/R does increase horsepower. Back in the late 1960's, Z-28 Camaros were running 11:1 C/R and L-88 Corvettes were even higher, but premium gas with very high octane ratings were readily available. With todays poor quality available pump gasoline, the highest C/R on a street engine (not talking race cars here) using cast iron heads is probably about 9:1, maybe up to 9.5:1 if you are using aluminum heads (because aluminum transfers heat faster than cast iron). Using a higher C/R without using high octane gas will produce pre-detonation (pinging) and this detonation will destroy an engine prematurely (like blowing a hole in the top of a piston). Cars nowadays use 8:1 C/R so they can use regular gas. Even with 9:1, you'll have to use the best premium gas available all the time (91 or 92 octane preferably). Hope this clears up some of the confusion.
Z||000016||spike||06-21-99||07:15 AM||||I am reading alot about 2.8, 3.1, 3.2 & 3.4 engine swaps in a Fiero. I read about a guy who installed a 87 buick 3.8 in his Fiero. He opted to make this engine a carb. set-up by installing a Edelbrook intake. He also did some porting on the heads and installed a better cam. He did not give any performance numbers so I am not sure of the hp he is producing. This is the type of install I 	would like to do. I think changing to a carb. set-up would eliminated some of the electrical hassels. What if you went one step further and stroked this 3.8 engine to like a 4.1 or more if possible. I dont see why you could not get at least 250 hp from this set-up. I dont think there are any high performance parts for this type of engine such as heads. Will parts from the 3.8 gn fit on this motor and would it help out on the hp side.
Z||000017||batboy||06-21-99||11:24 AM||||By the way, I'm not trying to put anyone down or talk you guys out of doing the engine swap of your choice. Just trying to be practical and give you good folks some food for thought. If you have deep pockets, then go for it. For all you power freaks out there (I'm one too) remember the old saying, there is no replacement for displacement. In other words, the bigger the engine, the easier it is to make more power (assuming high performance parts are readily available for it).<p>Spike, I know nothing about the Buick 3.8 liter. But, I'm in total agreement with you about going with a performance manifold and carburetor. Fuel injection gives you better fuel economy and burns cleaner, but is a lot more hassle in the horsepower chase. Assuming local laws allow it, going to a carb set-up lets you eliminate all the smog crap and complex computer sensors too (don't try this in California).<p>Since we're discussing possible swaps and if money is no problem, why not go with that 3800 Pontiac supercharged engine they put in those Grand Prix GTX's. How about turbo charging a 3.1 or 3.4, now that would be a screamer. I've never heard of anyone swapping in a Chevy 4.3 liter V6, but I'd think that would be a great engine to use. The 4.3 is basically a V8 Chevy 350 that is missing 2 cylinders. You'd have to use a V8 conversion kit, but there lots of those 4.3's around and there are tons of high performance parts for that engine. It would fit better and is lighter than a V8 too. With a little pocket porting on the heads, running a Crane Maximum Velocity cam, headers, etc., that 4.3 would put out at least 250 ponies and best of all, 280 ft/lbs of torque. Just some ideas, have fun.
Z||000018||fierosound||11-26-1999||01:53 PM||||Batboy...  in an earlier post you stated that one would be better off using a roller cam in a V6 to create power.  I only know of the GM roller cam (no specs available for it).  I don't know of any other cam manufacturers making roller cams suitable for the 2.8/3.1/3,4 V6 engine.  Do you?  If so, please post so I can check them out.  Thanks...<p>[This message has been edited by fierosound (edited 11-26-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000019||Phil||11-26-1999||06:33 PM||||I vote for the 3800 SC  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000020||batboy||11-26-1999||09:08 PM||||Well...nothing like an old post from the past haunting me. To be honest, all my engine hotrodding experience has been with V8's. I knew that a Chevy small block could easily be retro-fitted to a roller cam. I looked several places, but alas, all I could find in the way of V6 2.8/3.1 roller cams was this crane racing mechanical roller cam (which I do not recommend). You'd have to use fully ported racing heads, and of course, no computer. Does anyone else know of a good hydraulic roller cam for the V6?<P> <A HREF="http://www.cranecams.com/cgi-bin/spec.cgi?database=SPECCARD.tab&form=CardSamp.htm&id=258151" TARGET=_blank>http://www.cranecams.com/cgi-bin/spec.cgi?database=SPECCARD.tab&form=CardSamp.htm&id=258151</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000021||Terrybogin||11-26-1999||09:36 PM||||Didn't the later model HO 2.8's come with roller cams? ||||reg||1
Z||000022||chris6878||11-26-1999||11:26 PM||||Hey Guys...<P>this 3800 V6  is the same as the 3.8 v6 right?   My friend has a buick from the 80's which has a 3.8 v6 with carburator.  If this is the same engine.. wouldnt this be an easier swap considering you may bypass the computer and have less problems....      thanks for any info<P>chris..||||reg||3
Z||000023||88GT||11-27-1999||01:39 AM||||I also vote for the 3800 SC Series II.  I am planning on doing that swap when I start my project.  ||||reg||1
Z||000024||WKDFIRO||11-27-1999||04:01 AM||||Yikes theres a lot to say.<P>1)The 3800 and the GNX 3.8 are completely different. We've seen plenty of great 3800/3800SC conversion but not one 3.8 Buick.<BR>2)Only the 4 cyl go the roller lifters in 87-88.<BR>3)3.4 is rated at 160 hp and 200 lb-ft torque.<BR>4)2.8 blocks were used as FWD and RWD applications. The 3.4 in the f-bodies were only used in RWD. The 3.4 used in the the Transport have aluminum heads and are different blocks.||||reg||1
